WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.833
Welcome back. This is session four,
Learning Design.

2
00:00:07.833 --> 00:00:12.042
You'll find the session summary on page
seventeen of your participant

3
00:00:12.042 --> 00:00:12.521
guide.

4
00:00:12.521 --> 00:00:17.159
Have your classroom day map, rhythm audit
and carry into design

5
00:00:17.159 --> 00:00:19.463
card from session three beside you.

6
00:00:19.463 --> 00:00:22.864
We're going to use what you noticed to
make one small testable

7
00:00:22.864 --> 00:00:24.769
change in a classroom routine.

8
00:00:24.769 --> 00:00:25.787
The direction matters.

9
00:00:25.787 --> 00:00:29.417
We begin with the students and their
experience,

10
00:00:29.417 --> 00:00:32.829
then consider what can change in the
environment, the task or

11
00:00:32.829 --> 00:00:33.726
the routine.

12
00:00:34.524 --> 00:00:37.876
What we know about a student is useful
knowledge for design.

13
00:00:37.876 --> 00:00:41.427
It helps us understand each student in
context.

14
00:00:41.427 --> 00:00:46.434
Keep your reduce item in view, the barrier
or unnecessary demand

15
00:00:46.434 --> 00:00:47.790
you chose to explore.

16
00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:50.264
We'll work with others to design a
response,

17
00:00:50.264 --> 00:00:53.366
then each of you will check it against the
two or three students

18
00:00:53.366 --> 00:00:54.413
you have in mind.

19
00:00:54.413 --> 00:00:58.603
The aim is something practical enough to
try, observe and refine.

20
00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:06.000
Students come first. Before choosing a
strategy, return to the real students and

21
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.457
the evidence on your day map. What matters
to them? What is the specific

22
00:01:10.457 --> 00:01:15.417
access question? What have you observed,
and what are you still wondering? We’ll

23
00:01:15.417 --> 00:01:19.937
design for one classroom routine, then
test that design against different

24
00:01:19.937 --> 00:01:24.708
students. Keep an observation separate
from an interpretation, and a question

25
00:01:24.708 --> 00:01:29.794
separate from a conclusion. If you don’t
know how a student experiences something,

26
00:01:29.794 --> 00:01:33.874
leave room to find out through
consultation. A useful design brief

27
00:01:33.874 --> 00:01:38.708
connects what matters for students with
what we can change in the environment,

28
00:01:38.708 --> 00:01:43.229
task or routine. It keeps individual
knowledge in view without reducing a

29
00:01:43.229 --> 00:01:45.110
person to their support needs.

30
00:01:46.641 --> 00:01:51.729
Here’s how the session runs. We’ll regroup
into trios, write a brief, and

31
00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:57.171
consider a short menu of options. Then
we’ll apply a universal design lens and

32
00:01:57.172 --> 00:02:02.754
finish one practical design change. After
that, each person will privately check

33
00:02:02.755 --> 00:02:07.701
the design against their own students.
That check will take us into the

34
00:02:07.702 --> 00:02:13.497
continuum of support. We’ll finish with a
gallery and a personal commitment. So the

35
00:02:13.498 --> 00:02:19.009
rhythm is short inputs, then doing. The
trio creates one design together, while

36
00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:24.593
each of you keeps your own students in
view. By the end, you’ll have one routine

37
00:02:24.594 --> 00:02:29.328
redesigned, checked against real students,
and one thing to try. The

38
00:02:29.329 --> 00:02:34.346
recording has brief pauses at the activity
points; pause it for the full

39
00:02:34.347 --> 00:02:36.748
working time whenever you need to.

40
00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:43.383
Find the Reduce item on your Carry into
design card. Which kind of routine does

41
00:02:43.384 --> 00:02:48.619
it belong to: a rotation, a transition or
a whole-class routine? If you’re in the

42
00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:53.788
workshop room, move to the corner that
matches your routine type and form a trio

43
00:02:53.789 --> 00:02:58.827
with two people nearby. If a corner is
crowded, make several trios. If someone

44
00:02:58.828 --> 00:03:03.866
is on their own, welcome them into a
nearby group. Each trio needs one A three

45
00:03:03.867 --> 00:03:08.447
sheet, and each person needs a My students
card. Bring your day map and

46
00:03:08.448 --> 00:03:13.421
audit with you. Each of you will keep your
own two or three students in mind,

47
00:03:13.422 --> 00:03:18.459
even though you’ll design together. Allow
a few minutes to move and settle. If

48
00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:23.629
you’re following independently, choose one
of those three routine types and work

49
00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:28.341
through the design for your setting. Pause
the video while you get ready.

50
00:03:36.361 --> 00:03:41.958
Let’s settle into our groups. Check that
your trio has an A three sheet and that

51
00:03:41.959 --> 00:03:46.848
each of you has your own My students card.
You’ll agree on one routine

52
00:03:46.849 --> 00:03:52.446
shortly. For now, the shared routine type
gives you a place to begin; it doesn’t

53
00:03:52.447 --> 00:03:56.840
mean all your students experience that
routine in the same way.

54
00:03:58.361 --> 00:04:03.647
Before we begin, bring a concise picture
of your students back into view. Include

55
00:04:03.648 --> 00:04:08.934
their strengths, interests and sources of
joy. Think about communication, sensory

56
00:04:08.935 --> 00:04:14.023
experience and thinking; what costs and
what lightens; identity and belonging.

57
00:04:14.024 --> 00:04:18.716
Include what students, families and the
team already know, alongside the

58
00:04:18.717 --> 00:04:23.672
questions still open. These are prompts to
hold in mind, not another form to

59
00:04:23.673 --> 00:04:28.893
complete. We’re not building a file on one
child, and we’re not ranking students

60
00:04:28.894 --> 00:04:33.916
by need. We’re keeping real, different
people in view as we design. Later, in

61
00:04:33.917 --> 00:04:39.071
the private check, you’ll return to each
of these students and consider how the

62
00:04:39.072 --> 00:04:41.780
same design may work differently for them.

63
00:04:43.321 --> 00:04:47.614
Now agree on one routine your trio
recognises. It might come from one

64
00:04:47.615 --> 00:04:52.476
person’s Reduce card. Keep it specific
enough to work on today. On section one

65
00:04:52.477 --> 00:04:57.338
of your A three sheet, complete three
sentences together. First: ‘This routine

66
00:04:57.339 --> 00:05:01.947
asks students to...’ Describe what the
environment or task requires. Second:

67
00:05:01.948 --> 00:05:06.935
‘For some students, that means...’ Connect
the demand with what you know, and mark

68
00:05:06.936 --> 00:05:11.481
what is still a question. Third: ‘We could
change...’ Name something within

69
00:05:11.482 --> 00:05:16.154
educator or school influence. The same
prompts are on page eighteen of your

70
00:05:16.155 --> 00:05:20.637
Participant Guide. Check your wording
before you finish. Is it specific,

71
00:05:20.638 --> 00:05:25.436
workable and free from blame or deficit
labels? A phrase about what a student

72
00:05:25.437 --> 00:05:30.550
won’t do may need to become a description
of what the routine asks them to manage.

73
00:05:30.551 --> 00:05:35.665
Allow about eight minutes. You’re writing
one brief to keep active for the rest of

74
00:05:35.666 --> 00:05:39.326
this session. Pause the video and work
through it together.

75
00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:52.355
Let’s bring that first stage to a close.
Read your three sentences back to one

76
00:05:52.356 --> 00:05:57.352
another. Can you point to the demand in
the routine and the change within your

77
00:05:57.353 --> 00:06:02.218
influence? Have any interpretations
slipped in as facts? If you need to know

78
00:06:02.219 --> 00:06:07.345
more from a student, family or colleague,
keep that question visible. Your brief

79
00:06:07.346 --> 00:06:12.146
can be useful without pretending that
every part of the picture is settled.

80
00:06:12.147 --> 00:06:15.780
Leave it in front of you as we look at
possible supports.

81
00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:22.220
Here are four options to consider with
your brief in view: executive functioning

82
00:06:22.221 --> 00:06:27.182
supports, predictability, schedules and
visual supports. The option cards on your

83
00:06:27.183 --> 00:06:32.021
table carry these headings and the key
questions, so you can hold and sort them

84
00:06:32.022 --> 00:06:36.735
after the inputs. As you listen, keep
returning to your first sentence: ‘This

85
00:06:36.736 --> 00:06:41.388
routine asks students to...’ Which option
might answer that particular demand?

86
00:06:41.389 --> 00:06:45.916
We’ll choose one or two, rather than
trying to collect every strategy. The

87
00:06:45.917 --> 00:06:50.445
value is in the match between the support
and the access question you have

88
00:06:50.446 --> 00:06:51.127
identified.

89
00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:57.315
Executive functioning supports can make
the work of starting, remembering,

90
00:06:57.316 --> 00:07:02.056
sequencing, organising and shifting easier
to manage. Start with the demand

91
00:07:02.057 --> 00:07:07.181
in your routine, then ask what would help
with that demand. If getting started is

92
00:07:07.182 --> 00:07:11.601
difficult, could the first step be visible
and clear? If the task asks

93
00:07:11.602 --> 00:07:16.662
students to hold several instructions in
mind, could the sequence stay available

94
00:07:16.663 --> 00:07:21.466
to return to? If organising time and
materials takes a great deal of effort,

95
00:07:21.467 --> 00:07:26.143
could the task make those easier to locate
and manage? If the challenge is

96
00:07:26.144 --> 00:07:31.396
moving between tasks, what information or
preparation would make the shift clearer?

97
00:07:31.397 --> 00:07:36.392
These are support examples to consider,
not a package every student needs. Look

98
00:07:36.393 --> 00:07:41.389
back at your day map and brief. Which
demand are you actually addressing? Would

99
00:07:41.390 --> 00:07:46.194
the support reduce unnecessary effort, or
add another thing the student must

100
00:07:46.195 --> 00:07:51.126
manage? Then consider how the student can
show or tell you whether it helps. A

101
00:07:51.127 --> 00:07:56.187
resource can look organised to an adult
and still be confusing or burdensome for

102
00:07:56.188 --> 00:08:01.120
its intended user. We’re looking for
better access and participation, with the

103
00:08:01.121 --> 00:08:05.988
student’s experience helping us judge
usefulness. More compliance pressure is

104
00:08:05.989 --> 00:08:06.757
not the aim.

105
00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:13.336
Predictability means having accessible
information about routine and change. It

106
00:08:13.337 --> 00:08:18.653
doesn’t mean every day must be identical,
or that we can promise nothing unexpected

107
00:08:18.654 --> 00:08:23.192
will happen. Think about how much
information in a primary classroom is

108
00:08:23.193 --> 00:08:27.731
spoken once, implied, or held in an
adult’s head. If I couldn’t rely on

109
00:08:27.732 --> 00:08:32.918
remembering what someone had told me, how
would I know what was happening here? A

110
00:08:32.919 --> 00:08:37.975
useful support helps students answer four
questions: what is happening, what is

111
00:08:37.976 --> 00:08:43.163
changing, what comes next, and what can I
do if the plan changes? The information

112
00:08:43.164 --> 00:08:47.701
needs to arrive early enough to be useful.
Imagine an assembly has been

113
00:08:47.702 --> 00:08:52.694
moved earlier. How will students find out?
Has the change been made clear on a

114
00:08:52.695 --> 00:08:57.816
schedule or another accessible support?
Can they return to that information, see

115
00:08:57.817 --> 00:09:02.420
what follows, and ask a question or
request help in their preferred way?

116
00:09:02.421 --> 00:09:07.153
Another spoken announcement may not answer
all those needs. Return to your

117
00:09:07.154 --> 00:09:12.016
rhythm audit. When something in that
rhythm changes, how do students know? A

118
00:09:12.017 --> 00:09:17.009
visual support is one option, but the
format needs to fit the people using it.

119
00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:20.121
Review it through actual experience and
feedback.

120
00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:26.426
A schedule can make sequence, timing and
change visible. It gives students

121
00:09:26.427 --> 00:09:31.409
information they can return to, rather
than relying entirely on listening and

122
00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:36.064
remembering. Schedules can cover different
spans: a first-then sequence,

123
00:09:36.065 --> 00:09:40.982
one activity, part of the day or the whole
day. Choose a format the intended

124
00:09:40.983 --> 00:09:46.162
student can access and understand. The
question is whether it shows their actual

125
00:09:46.163 --> 00:09:51.014
day, including choices, pauses and
changes, or only the tasks adults expect

126
00:09:51.015 --> 00:09:55.735
them to complete. Make flexibility visible
too. If something changes, how

127
00:09:55.736 --> 00:10:00.587
does the schedule show that? Does it still
provide useful information after

128
00:10:00.588 --> 00:10:05.374
the original plan has changed? Can the
student see what comes next without

129
00:10:05.375 --> 00:10:10.029
needing another spoken explanation? A
schedule is useful when it reduces

130
00:10:10.030 --> 00:10:15.143
uncertainty and supports participation for
the person using it. Its presence on

131
00:10:15.144 --> 00:10:20.192
the wall or desk isn’t enough. Watch it in
use, invite feedback, and adjust it

132
00:10:20.193 --> 00:10:23.405
when it no longer answers the student’s
questions.

133
00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:29.702
With any visual support, start by asking
what the student needs to find,

134
00:10:29.703 --> 00:10:34.619
understand, remember or communicate. Then
choose a form that can make that

135
00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:39.604
information clear. It might be an object,
photograph, symbol, written word,

136
00:10:39.605 --> 00:10:44.925
checklist, map or choice support. Visual
does not automatically mean accessible.

137
00:10:44.926 --> 00:10:50.179
The student needs to understand what it
represents and be able to use it at the

138
00:10:50.180 --> 00:10:55.433
point of need. Keep unnecessary detail
out, explain how it works, and check its

139
00:10:55.434 --> 00:11:00.620
meaning with the student. Page nineteen
gives four quick-review questions. Can

140
00:11:00.621 --> 00:11:05.403
the student understand it? Can they access
it when they need it? Does it

141
00:11:05.404 --> 00:11:10.253
reflect what is actually happening,
including change? Does it give useful

142
00:11:10.254 --> 00:11:15.372
information or a way to respond? Keep
understanding and expressing in view. A

143
00:11:15.373 --> 00:11:20.155
visual support may help someone
understand, while A A C provides ways to

144
00:11:20.156 --> 00:11:25.544
communicate. Access to A A C doesn’t mean
every spoken instruction or schedule is

145
00:11:25.545 --> 00:11:30.057
understood. Keep the student’s
communication system available during

146
00:11:30.058 --> 00:11:35.176
routines, transitions and changes, and
recognise responses beyond speech. The

147
00:11:35.177 --> 00:11:40.228
Visual Supports Checklist and the online
webinar offer further learning. The

148
00:11:40.229 --> 00:11:45.213
checklist is a set of questions for
personalisation and consultation, not a

149
00:11:45.214 --> 00:11:50.669
prescription. Keep asking: does this give
this student useful information they can

150
00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:52.690
understand, access and act on?

151
00:11:54.200 --> 00:11:58.881
Return to your brief and the option cards.
Which one or two options answer

152
00:11:58.882 --> 00:12:03.305
it? You’ve heard about executive
functioning supports, predictability,

153
00:12:03.306 --> 00:12:08.500
schedules and visual supports. Sort those
possibilities against the demand in your

154
00:12:08.501 --> 00:12:13.310
routine. Choose one or two. For each,
write down why it fits: which specific

155
00:12:13.311 --> 00:12:18.376
demand does it answer? Record the choice
and your reason on section two of the A

156
00:12:18.377 --> 00:12:23.314
three sheet. You can also follow the
prompts on page eighteen. Give yourselves

157
00:12:23.315 --> 00:12:28.316
about three minutes. If everything seems
useful, return to the brief and narrow

158
00:12:28.317 --> 00:12:33.062
the choice. We’re making a manageable
response to a known demand. Pause the

159
00:12:33.063 --> 00:12:34.601
video for your decision.

160
00:12:42.640 --> 00:12:47.604
Let’s come back. Check that you have a
reason beside each option. Saying that a

161
00:12:47.605 --> 00:12:52.504
support is often helpful is different from
saying what it will make clearer or

162
00:12:52.505 --> 00:12:56.896
easier in this routine. Keep that
connection visible. It will help you

163
00:12:56.897 --> 00:13:01.415
explain the design, decide what to
observe, and revise the support if it

164
00:13:01.416 --> 00:13:03.770
doesn’t help in the way you expected.

165
00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:10.500
Before you finish the design, let’s apply
a Universal Design for Learning lens. Can

166
00:13:10.501 --> 00:13:15.658
you make important information clear and
available? Can you offer workable ways to

167
00:13:15.659 --> 00:13:20.433
participate and respond? Can you support
engagement while keeping meaningful

168
00:13:20.434 --> 00:13:25.463
challenge in the learning? Knowledge of
individual students and universal design

169
00:13:25.464 --> 00:13:30.366
can inform each other. Something you
understand about one student’s access may

170
00:13:30.367 --> 00:13:35.205
reveal a barrier worth reducing more
broadly. A broadly available support may

171
00:13:35.206 --> 00:13:39.980
then help several students in different
ways. But the presence of a resource

172
00:13:39.981 --> 00:13:44.756
doesn’t guarantee access. Offering another
format only helps if students can

173
00:13:44.757 --> 00:13:49.659
find it, understand it and use it. We’ll
now examine your emerging design with

174
00:13:49.660 --> 00:13:53.670
four practical questions, then test it
through use and feedback.

175
00:13:55.200 --> 00:14:00.183
Use section three of your A three sheet,
with the prompts on page nineteen. Run

176
00:14:00.184 --> 00:14:04.974
four questions over your draft design. Can
students find and understand what

177
00:14:04.975 --> 00:14:09.702
they need? Can they take part in more than
one way? Can they pause, ask for

178
00:14:09.703 --> 00:14:14.558
help or recover? Is the support available
when demand increases? Consider the

179
00:14:14.559 --> 00:14:19.095
details. If help is only available by
asking aloud, is that workable for

180
00:14:19.096 --> 00:14:23.503
everyone using this routine? If
information is useful at the start but

181
00:14:23.504 --> 00:14:28.614
disappears during a transition, will it be
there when needed? These are questions

182
00:14:28.615 --> 00:14:33.662
to test against your setting, rather than
assumptions about a student. Tick each

183
00:14:33.663 --> 00:14:38.518
question as you consider it. Where the
answer is ‘not yet’, adjust the design

184
00:14:38.519 --> 00:14:43.182
before moving on. Allow about six minutes.
Pause the video while your trio

185
00:14:43.183 --> 00:14:45.354
completes the accessibility check.

186
00:14:53.360 --> 00:14:58.152
Come back to the changes you made. The
check should influence the design, not

187
00:14:58.153 --> 00:15:02.189
just record that you discussed
accessibility. Keep any unresolved

188
00:15:02.190 --> 00:15:06.982
question beside the plan. This is an
initial check. You’ll still need to find

189
00:15:06.983 --> 00:15:11.901
out what happens when students use the
support and have a way to give feedback.

190
00:15:11.902 --> 00:15:16.379
Access is demonstrated through experience,
rather than guaranteed by the

191
00:15:16.380 --> 00:15:18.019
resource we have prepared.

192
00:15:19.520 --> 00:15:24.299
Now finish one practical change on section
four of your A three sheet. Page

193
00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:29.208
twenty of the Participant Guide carries
the design prompts. Work through five

194
00:15:29.209 --> 00:15:34.311
steps. State the barrier or unnecessary
demand. Describe what will change in the

195
00:15:34.312 --> 00:15:39.220
environment, task or routine. Include
students’ communication and experience.

196
00:15:39.221 --> 00:15:44.193
Decide what you will observe. Then set a
time to review. Keep the change small

197
00:15:44.194 --> 00:15:48.844
enough to try. You might change
information, timing, materials or the way

198
00:15:48.845 --> 00:15:53.882
the routine is organised, depending on
your brief. Be clear about what you hope

199
00:15:53.883 --> 00:15:58.791
students will be able to access or do more
readily. The measure of success is

200
00:15:58.792 --> 00:16:03.764
access, rather than compliance. Near the
end, ask a neighbouring trio to check

201
00:16:03.765 --> 00:16:08.996
that your design remains student-centred
and focused on what educators can change.

202
00:16:08.997 --> 00:16:14.035
If you’re working alone, read it again
from those two perspectives. Prepare one

203
00:16:14.036 --> 00:16:19.137
sentence describing the change and the
access question it addresses. Allow about

204
00:16:19.138 --> 00:16:23.465
ten minutes. Pause the video while you
develop and check the design.

205
00:16:31.480 --> 00:16:36.122
Let’s finish the sentence you’re writing
and come back together. You should have a

206
00:16:36.123 --> 00:16:40.478
change you can describe, something to
observe, and a time to review it. Check

207
00:16:40.479 --> 00:16:45.006
that student communication and experience
are included in how you will judge it.

208
00:16:45.007 --> 00:16:49.592
Hold onto your one-sentence description.
Now we’re going to see what happens when

209
00:16:49.593 --> 00:16:53.891
one shared design meets the different
students each of you has been thinking

210
00:16:53.892 --> 00:16:54.235
about.

211
00:16:55.760 --> 00:17:00.831
This next part is private. Take your own
My students card and turn to page

212
00:17:00.832 --> 00:17:05.765
twenty-one if you’re using the Participant
Guide. Use Student A, Student

213
00:17:05.766 --> 00:17:10.907
B and Student C, without names or
identifying details. Consider your trio’s

214
00:17:10.908 --> 00:17:16.396
design against each student. Mark Helps,
No difference, or Needs something more.

215
00:17:16.397 --> 00:17:21.607
Treat this as a working judgement based on
what you know, and keep questions

216
00:17:21.608 --> 00:17:26.749
visible where you need the student’s or
family’s perspective. Between three

217
00:17:26.750 --> 00:17:32.100
people, a trio may be holding six to nine
different students in view. The same

218
00:17:32.101 --> 00:17:37.658
design may work differently for each. You
can share only what you choose, perhaps

219
00:17:37.659 --> 00:17:43.148
how many Needs something more marks the
design collected, without describing the

220
00:17:43.149 --> 00:17:48.290
students. Allow about seven minutes for
your private check and any optional

221
00:17:48.291 --> 00:17:51.069
sharing. Pause the video while you work.

222
00:17:59.080 --> 00:18:03.884
Let’s return to the shared design. Where
it helps, the universal approach is doing

223
00:18:03.885 --> 00:18:07.799
useful work. Where it makes no difference,
or where a student needs

224
00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:12.544
something more, we have an important next
question. That doesn’t make the student

225
00:18:12.545 --> 00:18:17.290
the problem, and it doesn’t necessarily
make the whole design a failure. It shows

226
00:18:17.291 --> 00:18:21.561
us where targeted support or an individual
adjustment may be needed. Keep

227
00:18:21.562 --> 00:18:26.247
those Needs something more marks in mind
as we look at the continuum of support.

228
00:18:27.760 --> 00:18:32.483
Support is a continuum, rather than one
intervention that answers every need.

229
00:18:32.484 --> 00:18:37.270
Start with an inclusive environment:
predictable routines, clear expectations,

230
00:18:37.271 --> 00:18:41.621
accessible information, and a classroom
culture where different ways of

231
00:18:41.622 --> 00:18:45.972
participating are accepted and valued.
Then consider universal supports

232
00:18:45.973 --> 00:18:50.759
available to many students. Instructions
can be offered in spoken, written and

233
00:18:50.760 --> 00:18:55.359
visual forms. Students may have flexible
ways to demonstrate learning, with

234
00:18:55.360 --> 00:18:59.772
movement breaks or sensory tools available
when useful. Targeted support

235
00:18:59.773 --> 00:19:04.621
responds to a more specific need. For
example, a small group might benefit from

236
00:19:04.622 --> 00:19:09.532
key vocabulary being introduced before a
lesson, or from additional organisation

237
00:19:09.533 --> 00:19:13.821
support during independent work.
Individual adjustments respond to the

238
00:19:13.822 --> 00:19:17.924
particular student’s access and
participation needs. Examples might

239
00:19:17.925 --> 00:19:22.586
include a quieter workspace for an
assessment, additional processing time, a

240
00:19:22.587 --> 00:19:27.186
personalised task breakdown or another way
to demonstrate learning. What is

241
00:19:27.187 --> 00:19:32.096
appropriate depends on the student and the
context, informed by consultation and

242
00:19:32.097 --> 00:19:37.132
evidence. Look back at the design check.
What is available to everyone? What might

243
00:19:37.133 --> 00:19:40.986
need to be targeted? What needs individual
adjustment? Academic

244
00:19:40.987 --> 00:19:45.461
adjustments sit within this wider picture.
A diagnosis alone doesn’t tell

245
00:19:45.462 --> 00:19:50.310
us which adjustment will help. Connect the
response with your observations, the

246
00:19:50.311 --> 00:19:54.786
day map, and student and family knowledge,
then review it through use and

247
00:19:54.787 --> 00:19:55.345
feedback.

248
00:19:56.880 --> 00:20:01.868
Reasonable adjustments are changes or
supports that help students access and

249
00:20:01.869 --> 00:20:06.391
participate in education in a way that
works for them. They should be

250
00:20:06.392 --> 00:20:11.180
individualised, build on strengths, remove
barriers, and be practical and

251
00:20:11.181 --> 00:20:16.036
effective. Informed decisions and review
help us find out whether they are

252
00:20:16.037 --> 00:20:21.091
enabling the student. The diagram places
adjustments within the wider work of

253
00:20:21.092 --> 00:20:26.479
environmental design and Universal Design
for Learning. It also names the four N C

254
00:20:26.480 --> 00:20:30.869
C D levels of adjustment: quality
differentiated teaching practice,

255
00:20:30.870 --> 00:20:35.991
supplementary, substantial and extensive.
Keep two things distinct. Reasonable

256
00:20:35.992 --> 00:20:41.313
adjustments are the changes and supports
provided. The N C C D is the process for

257
00:20:41.314 --> 00:20:46.701
collecting information about adjustments
for students with disability. Evidence of

258
00:20:46.702 --> 00:20:51.556
what has been provided and monitored
matters; a diagnostic label by itself

259
00:20:51.557 --> 00:20:56.878
doesn’t choose an adjustment or tell the
whole story. Within curriculum learning,

260
00:20:56.879 --> 00:21:01.201
we want support to build capability,
confidence, self-advocacy and

261
00:21:01.202 --> 00:21:06.390
independence. The question is whether
students can access and participate, with

262
00:21:06.391 --> 00:21:11.512
support that empowers them. For formal
requirements and decisions, use current

263
00:21:11.513 --> 00:21:15.037
official guidance and your school or
system processes.

264
00:21:16.560 --> 00:21:21.437
For N C C D information, use the current
official guidance alongside your school

265
00:21:21.438 --> 00:21:25.451
or system’s consultation, documentation
and review processes. Make

266
00:21:25.452 --> 00:21:30.267
responsibilities clear to the student,
family and staff involved: what is being

267
00:21:30.268 --> 00:21:34.898
tried, who will put it in place, what
evidence will be gathered, and when it

268
00:21:34.899 --> 00:21:39.344
will be reviewed. Keep the connection to
the educational access question.

269
00:21:39.345 --> 00:21:44.161
Documentation should help explain the
student’s needs, the adjustments provided

270
00:21:44.162 --> 00:21:48.915
and what is learned from reviewing them.
It shouldn’t replace listening to the

271
00:21:48.916 --> 00:21:53.423
student or family. Today’s design work
gives you a starting point for that

272
00:21:53.424 --> 00:21:57.869
conversation. It doesn’t replace the
formal processes in your setting. In

273
00:21:57.870 --> 00:22:02.747
Session Five we’ll develop consultation
and partnership further, including the N

274
00:22:02.748 --> 00:22:06.946
C C D meeting tool kit, so keep your open
questions with your design.

275
00:22:08.480 --> 00:22:13.408
It’s time to see what other trios have
